<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sikandar Lodi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1489">1489</a>–<a href="/years/1517">1517</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sikandar · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sikandar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> was a sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> of the Lodi dynasty, who ruled in the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries. He strengthened the sultanate and founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> as a capital. The Lodi recovery under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahlul-lodi">Bahlul</a> and Sikandar was genuine but limited, never regaining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi.jpg" alt="Sikandar Lodi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anupamg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Sikandar_Lodi_028.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sikandar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> was born Nizamudin Loadu on 17 July 1458, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahlul-lodi">Bahlul Lodi</a>, who had founded the Lodi dynasty. He became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> after his father's death in July 1489 and ruled until his own death on 21 November 1517. He was the second and most successful Lodi ruler, a capable administrator who encouraged trade across his territory. He extended Lodi rule into Gwalior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, made a treaty with Alauddin Hussain Shah and the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, and in 1503 commissioned the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>.</p><p>His main wars were against the Tomar rajas of Gwalior. In 1500 Raja Man Singh Tomar sheltered rebels from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and in 1501 Sikandar captured Dholpur, a dependency of Gwalior. In 1504 he resumed the war and took the Mandrayal fort, and from September 1505 to May 1506 he ravaged the countryside around Gwalior but could not take the fort against Man Singh's hit-and-run tactics. He then took smaller forts, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narwar">Narwar</a> after a year-long siege. An illness prevented his planned attack on Gwalior in 1516, and he died the next year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Sikandar patronised learning, and under his influence poetry and music revived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> poet Dungar taught at a Muslim college. He wrote Persian poetry under the pen name Gul Rukhi, 'rose-faced', and prepared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> of 9000 verses.</p><p>Because his mother was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, he adopted a strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy as a political expedient to prove his Islamic credentials. He destroyed Hindu temples, and under pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> allowed the execution of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> who declared Hinduism as true as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. He also established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> courts in several towns, open to the non-Muslim population as well, even for property disputes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sikandar Khan Lodi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lodi recovery under Bahlul and <b>Sikandar</b> was genuine but limited.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan <b>Sikandar</b> is remembered for destroying temples, while Zain al-Abidin, known as Budshah, the great king, restored tolerance, recalled Hindu Pandits, had Sanskrit works translated into Persian and built canals and bridges.</blockquote><a href="/state/kashmir-sultanate">Kashmir Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.67,77.22&amp;year=1503" aria-label="Delhi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6568 1115 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sikandar Lodi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7018 1385h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7018 1385h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=28.67,77.22&amp;year=1503">What was here in 1503? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
